  "summary": "Distinctive carpet motifs from the Tekke Turkmen tribe featuring geometric guls (medallions) and tribal symbols.",
  "description": "Characterized by bold, octagonal or hexagonal guls (medallions) with intricate inner patterns and tribal symbolism in red, blue, and ivory; developed by Tekke Turkmen weavers as essential household items and trade goods. The motifs encode tribal identity and cosmological beliefs.",
  "culturalContext": "Tekke carpet motifs — the Turkmen tribe's canonical weaving grammar (Tekke, Yomut, Saryk) — center on the güls: octagonal medallions (Tekke's \"Princess Bokhara\" güls, Yomut's linked crosses) whose count, color and kelim ends encoded tribal identity, rank and dowry value. Madder-red fields, ivory-ground borders, miniature ak-chul \"horse- neck\" bands and tent-band trifles (asanlyk) completed the program; the motif system is Central Asia's densest textile semiotics, documented by Oriental Carpet scholarship and still read by Turkmen weavers.",
